1
00:00:06,919 --> 00:00:15,082
JAMIE: Treasury yields just hit five percent again, and the Fed's about to make it worse—which means your mortgage just got a new hobby: getting more expensive.

2
00:00:15,082 --> 00:00:34,732
ALEX: Markets had a quiet day—S&P down point three-eight percent, Nasdaq down point seven-four—but the real story is happening inside the chip sector. AMD jumped thirteen point two-six percent while Nvidia dropped four point two-seven percent. Rates are up on the ten-year, and suddenly the trade is: own the companies that actually make things instead of the ones that just say they'll think about it.

3
00:00:34,732 --> 00:00:48,034
JAMIE: So we're rotating out of the pure AI hype plays into companies with, what, actual revenue streams? Broadcom's up eight point seven-eight percent too—is this the moment the market remembers that chips have to exist before you can run your model on them?

4
00:00:48,034 --> 00:01:03,755
MAYA: Yes, and the timing is wild because everyone's been treating Nvidia like a vending machine that dispenses earnings, but AMD and Broadcom are like, 'Hey, we also sell to people who aren't just training ChatGPT derivatives'—Meta's up five point one-two, Tesla up five point five-four, so the broadening out is real.

5
00:01:03,755 --> 00:01:13,429
MAYA: CoreWeave just beat on revenue and raised guidance, which is the first real signal that AI infrastructure demand isn't just a story LLM labs are telling to justify their burn rates.

6
00:01:13,429 --> 00:01:21,591
ALEX: When the company selling the shovels reports actual customer demand instead of theoretical TAM, the market notices. That's CoreWeave's beat—it means the infrastructure layer isn't vaporware.

7
00:01:21,591 --> 00:01:36,102
MAYA: All seven Anthropic founders are each cashing out about two hundred fifty million dollars in the IPO, and they're doing it in lockstep—same number of shares, same price, same 'we built this and we're taking it off the table' energy. That's not founder ego, that's founder clarity.
  ↳ source: https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/10/all-7-anthropic-founders-will-each-cash-out-about-250m-in-ipo/

8
00:01:36,102 --> 00:01:42,753
JAMIE: So they're not doing the thing where one founder sells a hundred million and another holds for the long-term mission statement?

9
00:01:42,753 --> 00:01:53,334
MAYA: Nope. Seven founders, seven identical exits. It reads like a press release from people who've already decided the narrative—'We made something real, we're getting paid, and we're not pretending this is a fifty-year crusade.'

10
00:01:53,334 --> 00:01:58,171
JAMIE: Sony just killed Fairgames, their live-service shooter. It's dead. The live-service graveyard gets another headstone.

11
00:01:58,171 --> 00:02:10,264
ALEX: Turns out you can't just fund a game to compete with free-to-play incumbents that already have millions of players. Sony learned what every other publisher keeps learning: capital alone doesn't move the needle when the audience has already chosen.

12
00:02:10,264 --> 00:02:22,356
ALEX: Credit card debt hit a record high, and rates are still elevated, which means Americans are borrowing just to keep pace with the cost of living—not to spend on anything new. That's the recession signal the market keeps ignoring.
  ↳ source: https://www.axios.com/2026/11/10/credit-card-debt-record-2026-delinquency-income-gap

13
00:02:22,356 --> 00:02:28,100
ALEX: Five percent yields mean every dollar borrowed today costs more tomorrow—and the Fed's got the gas pedal pinned.

14
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:31,123
MAYA: Your mortgage isn't getting expensive. It's getting *expensive* expensive.

15
00:02:31,123 --> 00:02:35,356
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
